<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Curt von François</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1852">1852</a>–<a href="/years/1931">1931</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Curt von François was a German army officer and explorer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. As commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">South West Africa</a>, he founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a> in 1890 as the colony's military and administrative centre. In 1893 he led an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-witbooi">Hendrik Witbooi</a>'s camp at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hornkranz">Hornkranz</a>, in which many women and children were killed, and he was replaced the following year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/curt-von-francois.jpg" alt="Curt von François" width="320" height="548" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CurtvonFrancois.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Curt Karl Bruno von François was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> into a family of French Huguenot ancestry. His father, the Prussian general Bruno von François, was killed at the Battle of Spicheren on 6 August 1870; his younger brother Hermann von François later served as a general in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and the writer Louise von François was his aunt. Curt joined the Prussian Cadet Corps and fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>, for which he received the Iron Cross.</p><p>He then turned to exploration. In 1883 he worked as a geographer on an expedition along the Kasai River in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-basin">Congo Basin</a> under Hermann Wissmann, and two years later he joined a second Congo expedition led by George Grenfell. After service on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">German General Staff</a>, where he rose to Hauptmann, he was sent to German West Africa in 1887 and explored Togoland and the trade route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salaga">Salaga</a> as far north as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Herero leaders expelled German officials from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okahandja">Okahandja</a> in 1888, the German Colonial Society engaged François to secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a>. He landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walvis-bay">Walvis Bay</a> in June 1889 with 21 troopers and based himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otjimbingwe">Otjimbingwe</a>, against the advice of the acting commissioner Heinrich Göring. In May 1890 he renewed a peace agreement with the Herero chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharero">Maharero</a> and occupied the ruined settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a>, where he built the headquarters known as the Alte Feste. He served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichskommissar">Reichskommissar</a> from March 1891 to November 1893, established Swakopmund as the colony's main harbour in 1892 and mapped much of the territory.</p><p>Fearing an alliance between the Herero and the Nama, he led 225 colonial troops against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-witbooi">Hendrik Witbooi</a>'s village on 12 April 1893. Witbooi withdrew with most of his fighters, and the attack killed 88 Nama, 78 of them women and children. Newspapers reported and condemned the massacre, while German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> officials tried to cover it up. Witbooi waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war from the Naukluft Mountains, and François failed to drive the Nama into the Omaheke Desert.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-leutwein">Theodor Leutwein</a> replaced him in 1894, and François retired from the army in 1895. Living at Zernsdorf in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, he wrote at length on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, including Deutsch-Südwest-Afrika (1899). He died in a hospital at Königs Wusterhausen on 28 December 1931 and was buried in the Invalids' Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>; his grave, levelled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a>'s border zone, was restored in 2018. A statue of him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a>, unveiled in 1965, was taken down on 23 November 2022 after repeated calls for its removal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Curt von François” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Protection treaties with the Herero Paramountcy and the Rehoboth Basters followed, and <b>Curt von François</b> founded Windhoek as the capital in 1890.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Luxembourg. Died in Königs Wusterhausen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.61,6.13&amp;year=1903" aria-label="Luxembourg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Curt von François"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5339 5868h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5339 5868h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.61,6.13&amp;year=1903">What was here in 1903? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
